    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "Revolt of 1857",
     "Doctrine of Lapse",
     "Rani Lakshmibai",
     "Indian National Congress",
     "Moderates and Extremists",
     "Swadeshi"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "The Revolt of 1857 began with sepoys at Meerut and spread across north and central India. Causes included greased cartridges, annexation by the Doctrine of Lapse, high taxes and loss of power for Indian rulers. Leaders were Rani Lakshmibai, Nana Sahib, Tantia Tope and Bahadur Shah Zafar.\nThe revolt failed due to lack of unity and British strength. After it, the rule of the Company ended and the British Crown took over in 1858.\nLater the Indian National Congress was founded in 1885 by A. O. Hume and others. The early leaders, called Moderates, used petitions, and the Extremists, like Tilak, wanted bolder action. The Swadeshi movement followed the Partition of Bengal in 1905."
    }
   },
